WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:12.110
As the day cools down, life slowly returns
to the bush in south-east Zimbabwe.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:17.980
The African wild dogs spent the hottest part
of the day sleeping in the shade.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:25.830
Now in the late afternoon, the pack comes
together ready to go out hunting.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.210
The perfect time for Jessica Watermeyer to
check up on the pack.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:35.649
She heads up a conservation project for wild
dogs in the Savé Valley Conservancy.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:39.940
It's a protected area that covers 3000 square
kilometers.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.969
She's spent years working with these animals.

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:45.969
This is our festive pack.

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:49.589
We've called them that because as you can
see they've got beautiful, very distinct and

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:50.589
colorful markings.

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:53.829
They also happen to be one of the biggest
packs in the conservancy.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:56.559
There are about 11 adults now.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:58.519
Started off the denning season, or the year,
with twelve.

00:00:58.519 --> 00:01:03.659
They had 14 puppies but we're down to seven
now and they're also obviously a very relaxed

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:04.839
and habituated pack as you can see.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:10.310
So fantastic for us working with them and
for monitoring and research purposes.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:15.420
Wild dogs are among the most endangered carnivores
in Africa, with population numbers of around

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.540
seven thousand at most.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.990
The Savé Valley Conservancy provides a large
area for them to roam freely, which is exactly

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.789
what they need to survive.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:31.030
Impala Antelopes are their first choice of
prey.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.079
"They are incredibly social pack animals.

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:40.210
Just the way they interact with each other,
the way they take care of each other, the

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.140
way they take care of the puppies, how all
pack members pull together to look after each

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.840
other - very different from other social carnivores
for example lions.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.179
When wild dogs had a kill the adults will
let the puppies eat first.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:54.250
They will look after the injured.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.210
They will take meat back to the injured."

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:03.499
The Savé Conservancy was originally formed
from an amalgamation of large private ranches.

00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:08.050
It finances itself through tourism and controlled
sustainable hunting.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:14.080
This means endangered species are protected
and the area is not sacrificed for farmland.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.800
Outside the conservancy, little of the original
bush remains.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.349
The local population has grown, and more and
more land has gone over to farming, leading

00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:27.290
to increased contact between wildlife and
humans.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:34.870
The conservationists now work closely with
the local communities to protect the animals.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:42.040
Victor Chibaya teaches school students about
how the wild animals live.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.879
He and his team visit the schools three times
a year.

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:49.849
Many of the children are fascinated.

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:55.090
The local villagers often have a very different
take on how to deal with the wildlife in their

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.790
area.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:04.689
The traditional knowledge around the conservancy
is, basically, they normally chase them away

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:12.010
if they are predators but if they are herbivores
they normally kill them for meat.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:17.340
So the knowledge which we are bringing now,
the scientific one, is, "No, we have to coexist

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:26.330
with even the large carnivores and also the
prey species as well."

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.920
Chibaya teaches the children about the food
chain - and explains what happens when one

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.360
species dies out completely.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:39.069
The idea is to help the children see the importance
of conservation.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:43.049
I think it's great to learn about protecting
the animals.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:50.610
And now I know that if poachers come I need
to report it to the police or my teachers.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:57.769
The wild dog project also finances books and
teaching materials and even pays the tuition

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:00.150
fees for some of the students.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.620
This school started working with the conservationists
in 2013.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.890
Headmaster Elia Ngwenya says a lot has changed
since then.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:14.780
You can actually see, that even if we see
a snake at the school as of now they will

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.930
tell you, "Oh, Victor Chibaya doesn't like
us to kill these things."

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.320
They say, "Please leave this thing.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.110
It's there to live because it's very important
for the ecosystem."

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:32.270
Of those kids that have actually passed through
this school we have never had a kid who has

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:38.080
been arrested for poaching.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:45.520
Back in the conservancy, the pack are hunting...
close to a kill.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.810
But this wildebeest is a little too big for
them.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:56.419
Still it provides good sparring partner for
the younger dogs to hone their hunting skills.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:05.620
So after a little excitement, predators and
potential prey go their separate ways.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:10.970
But African wild dogs are themselves exposed
to danger, even inside the park - as we discover

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.150
the next day.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.919
Ranger Cain Kodzevhu is out on patrol when
a colleague contacts him.

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:22.940
"They found a carcass of a wild dog dead from
a snare."

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:28.540
The ranger immediately heads to the scene,
where Jessica Watermeyer is waiting for him.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:33.190
It soon becomes clear that the situation is
even worse than they thought.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.819
We got a report this morning that there were
two dogs that were caught in wires.

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:40.220
They were still fighting the wires.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.520
So we rushed here as soon as possible and
got here within twenty minutes of the report.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.970
But unfortunately by the time we got here
the dogs had already strangled themselves

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:53.960
and suffocated.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.610
It's a senseless killing.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.620
The dogs were not even the target of the poachers.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.940
They set the wire traps to catch antelopes.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:07.910
The dogs tried to free themselves by biting
through the wire, but the copper was too tough.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:13.420
Worse still: it's the alpha male that's been
killed, along with another adult dog, the

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.190
best hunters of the pack.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:22.360
The next step will be for our scouts to work
with some of the local ranch scouts and to

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.290
move through this area now and sweep and make
sure we can pick up as many of these wires

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.870
as possible.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:30.720
Ideally you pick the wires up before this
happens.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.599
The wire is almost invisible in the dense
bush and the area is vast.

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:39.750
But the rangers manage to find around 50 wire
traps.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.080
Dogs are unique to Africa.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:42.860
You won't find them anywhere else.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:48.650
As conservationists working in Africa we have
a duty to protect them to make sure that they

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.289
stay here for future generations.

00:06:50.289 --> 00:06:54.069
And they are a completely genetically distinct
species.

00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:56.270
Once they're gone, they're gone.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.889
The rest of the pack will stick together until
the juveniles are fully grown.

00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:05.620
Fortunately the alpha female has survived
unscathed - otherwise the pack would have

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:06.620
broken up.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:12.229
But with her mate now gone, hopes of gaining
a new litter of puppies this year now look

00:07:12.229 --> 00:07:12.610
slim.

